<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ortygia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ortygia is a small island forming the historic centre of Syracuse in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, joined to the mainland by bridges. Corinthian colonists settled it in 734 or 733 BCE, drawn by its two harbours and the freshwater spring of Arethusa. It held the city's acropolis and temples, including the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> and the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> now incorporated into Syracuse Cathedral.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ortygia.jpg" alt="Ortygia" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Agostino Artnoir Sella · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ortigia_dall%27alto.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
